{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>For from the top of the rocks I see him</b> — The reason is that since he [Balak] brought him up to the high places of Baal to see him, he said: From the top of the rocks and from the hills I look and see him, that he shall dwell alone, with no other nation reckoned together with him, as many peoples and different nations gather to become one camp. But these all have one Torah and one law for them, and they are one nation, and they shall dwell alone in the name of Jacob and Israel. And therefore he mentioned \"How shall I curse, whom Jacob?\" and \"How shall I be enraged, whom Israel?\" (Numbers 23:7), for he mentioned to them their honored name and the names of their fathers, to say that they are a nation apart and their name befits them from their fathers. For Balak did not mention to him the name Israel, but only said \"a people has come out of Egypt\" (Numbers 22:5), as one feigning unfamiliarity with them so as not to acknowledge them, for he was ungrateful for the kindness of their father. And the intent is to say that just as I now see him dwelling alone, so shall he dwell for all eternity secure, alone — the eye of Jacob — and he shall be the head of the world, and there is no nation that shall prevail over him, nor shall he be subordinate to them:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:05.896914798Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}